**Theorem 6.3 (general-target single-body cycle).** For every target
in $\mathcal A_{\mathrm{gc}}$, each decoder on its authentic body returns
exactly $h_k,W(h_k)$. Each arrow terminates and emits the next independent
view of that same $h_k$. The composite returns the identical initial S
body. Each accepted canonical body re-encodes identically.

Proof. Lemma 3.3 gives the two shape inverses. Theorem 4.3 supplies
input completeness for their recovered raw record and the memory
body, including every general-target frontier. Reselection checks all
memory fields after replay, so partial callable annotations do not
justify their own recovery. For T, induction over checked records uses
their own supplied bits and Lemma 2.6. Lemma 5.3 supplies binding tests
for this exact acquired target. CYCLE's direct encoders then compute
the independent definitions, hence the next view and its canonical body.
Their existing finite path/list traversals and the decreasing replay
countdown prove termination. Composition gives the stated identity.
No original-task assertion is used in any of these steps. ∎

